\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{From move sequences to play}

  \p{A formal move description becomes useful only when a player can apply it
  and reproduce its result. Play gives the mathematics an immediate test of
  intelligibility: a notation should identify a move unambiguously, a
  displayed turn should agree with the stated action, and replaying a
  sequence should reproduce the same state. Scrambling, undoing, recognizing
  a case, and following a solution all depend on those agreements.}

  \p{The [tetrahedron turn](frap-000O) and
  [cube face turn](frap-000P) give worked actions to explore.
  Small interactive examples could accompany them:
  follow a piece through a turn, compare two orders of moves, or inspect
  what a sequence preserves. A fuller playing interface can then bring
  together notation, visualization, replay, and methods. An animation
  illustrates a claim; a proof must explain why it holds for the stated
  class of moves and states.}

  \p{The existing [Rubik's Cube resources](uts-016J) collect material on
  notation, methods, and demonstrations. The companion
  [FRAP repository](https://github.com/utensil/frap) will support later
  formal and executable work. The notes now develop incidence moves,
  framed orientations and transfer; the companion repository does not
  yet provide a formalized puzzle model or solver.}
